# SFU Server Configuration

# Port for the SFU server
PORT=5005

# Where servers register themselves. Separate from PORT on purpose: PORT is the one you
# publish so clients can reach it, and registration must not be reachable alongside it.
# Leave this unpublished — the Gryt server reaches it over the container network.
SFU_CONTROL_PORT=9092

# The address the control port listens on. Unset, it's every interface, which a
# server in another container needs. If the SFU and the server both run straight on
# one machine, set 127.0.0.1 so nobody else on the network can register. The desktop
# app does that. It has to be an IP address, or the SFU won't start.
# SFU_CONTROL_HOST=127.0.0.1

# STUN servers (comma separated)
STUN_SERVERS=stun:stun.l.google.com:19302

# The one UDP port every participant's media flows over. Open it on the SFU
# host firewall, and publish it if the SFU runs in a container.
#
# 3478 is the default, and the best of the bad options on a locked-down network.
# It is the IANA STUN port, it needs no privileged bind, and it is the UDP port
# corporate and school firewalls are most likely to have opened already, because
# Microsoft Teams requires outbound UDP 3478-3481 and Zoom uses it too.
#
# UDP 443 looks like the obvious choice and is not. Enterprise firewall vendors
# recommend blocking it: QUIC on UDP 443 cannot be TLS-inspected, so blocking it
# forces browsers back to TCP where it can be. On the networks you would pick it
# for, it is the port most likely to be deliberately shut.
#
# Worth knowing either way: ports are per-protocol. UDP 443 and TCP 443 are
# different sockets, so an HTTPS server never collides with media on UDP 443.
# HTTP/3 does, because that is UDP 443 as well.
#
# No UDP port is allowed everywhere, and Gryt does not paper over that with a
# relay. There is no TURN server here on purpose: a relay puts an extra hop
# between two people who could have talked directly, and pays for it in latency
# and bandwidth on every packet for the whole call. Gryt gets a direct path or
# it does not connect.
#
# So the port you forward is the thing that matters. A network that blocks
# outbound UDP outright cannot carry Gryt voice, and that is a deliberate trade
# rather than a missing feature.
# ICE_UDP_MUX_PORT=3478

# Optional: UDP port for Gryt's own voice transport (WebTransport over QUIC),
# which skips WebRTC for voice. Defaults to 4433. It only starts when
# ICE_ADVERTISE_IP is set, since clients dial that address. Open the port in
# your firewall, or set 0 and clients stay on WebRTC.
# AUDIO_TRANSPORT_PORT=4433

# Optional: UDP port for Gryt's native voice engine (phone and desktop apps), plain
# UDP with every packet encrypted. Off by default; it rides on the transport above.
# AUDIO_UDP_PORT=4434

# Optional: cap how many peers may be connected at once. Defaults to 200.
# This is a guardrail rather than a limit the port imposes — one muxed port
# carries more peers than a machine has CPU and upload bandwidth for. If you
# need more than one machine's worth, run a second SFU.
# MAX_PEERS=200

# Optional: force the IP address(es) the SFU advertises in ICE host candidates.
# Use this when the SFU is behind NAT, or when it must never hand out an address
# you did not choose.
#
# Comma-separated for multi-network (e.g. public + LAN); ICE generates a
# candidate for each and clients pick the best path.
#
# Setting this turns STUN off by default, because the two answer the same
# question and only one of them is under your control. See DISABLE_STUN below.
#
# Entries are checked at boot. Anything that is not an IP address is dropped
# with a warning, and a private or loopback address is kept but warned about,
# since a peer outside your network cannot reach one. A stale entry here is
# invisible otherwise: the SFU keeps working on its other candidates.
# ICE_ADVERTISE_IP=203.0.113.10
# ICE_ADVERTISE_IP=203.0.113.10,192.168.1.100

# Disable server-side STUN, which stops the SFU discovering its own public
# address and gathering a server-reflexive candidate.
#
# Defaults to true when ICE_ADVERTISE_IP is set, and false otherwise. Setting it
# explicitly wins either way.
#
# The default is that way round because discovery answers the same question as
# ICE_ADVERTISE_IP and gives a different answer when the network changes
# underneath. If the SFU's egress moves -- a tunnel drops and traffic falls back
# to the ordinary route -- STUN starts reporting the new address and the SFU
# hands that out instead. Calls keep connecting, so nothing surfaces it, and the
# address you are advertising is no longer the one you chose. That happened on
# gryt.chat's own deployment and went unnoticed for weeks (GRYT-768).
#
# Turning STUN back on alongside a forced address is reasonable when you want a
# fallback path and do not mind what it advertises. Leaving it on without a
# forced address is the normal case: something has to work out the public
# address, and STUN is the only thing that can.
#
# Note it is safe to leave off only when the path to the internet is direct and
# port-preserving (host networking, bare metal, 1:1 NAT). With Docker or cloud
# NAT the external port often differs from ICE_UDP_MUX_PORT, and then a
# forced-address-only candidate names a port nothing is listening on.
# DISABLE_STUN=true

# How often the SFU pings each WebSocket, in seconds, and how long a peer may
# say nothing at all before it is hung up on.
#
# The ping does two jobs. It notices a peer that went away without closing the
# connection (a shut laptop, LTE that dropped, a process killed behind a NAT
# that still holds the mapping open), which otherwise leaves the SFU forwarding
# to somebody who is not there and counting them against MAX_PEERS. And it is
# the only traffic that travels server to client while a call is quiet, so a
# firewall in between has a reason not to reap the connection as idle.
#
# It works where the client's own keep-alive does not. A browser throttles the
# timers of a backgrounded tab to roughly once a minute, so the message the
# client sends stops arriving exactly when the connection most needs traffic.
# A ping is answered below the JavaScript layer, on a tab doing nothing.
#
# SFU_PING_INTERVAL=0 switches both off, read deadline included. That is the
# escape hatch if this ever hangs up on people who were fine, and it needs no
# release. gryt_sfu_websocket_ping_timeouts_total counts how often it happens.
#
# A timeout under two intervals is raised to two, since anything less hangs up
# on a peer that lost a single pong.
# SFU_PING_INTERVAL=30
# SFU_PONG_TIMEOUT=90

# How long somebody may be the only person left in a call before the SFU ends
# it, in seconds.
#
# Only a call. A voice channel is a place, and sitting in one on your own is an
# ordinary thing to do — you are waiting for somebody, or you like having it
# open. A call is an event between named people, and being the last one in it
# means it is over: usually somebody walked off, sometimes a tab is open behind
# forty others. The room, its peer connection and its socket stay up either way
# until something ends them.
#
# The client ends its own and says so first. This is for the ones that never
# will: closed laptops, wedged tabs, anything modified. Those are exactly the
# clients leaving rooms up.
#
# Checked every 15 seconds, so a call ends between this and 15 seconds after
# it. 0 switches it off.
#
# Whatever this is set to is sent to each client in room_joined, so the
# countdown a person sees is this number rather than the client's own guess at
# it, and 0 stops the client hanging up on a call this SFU would have kept. A
# client can also send still_here to restart the clock, which is the "stay in
# the call" button; there is no cap on that, because somebody pressing it is
# somebody at a keyboard.
# SFU_CALL_ALONE_TIMEOUT=120

# The most each peer should send this SFU, in kbps. For an uplink shared by more
# people than the send estimate can see. Clients from @gryt/voice 0.6.1 keep
# their video under it; older ones ignore it, and the SFU drops nothing either way.
# SFU_MAX_INGEST_KBPS=

# Debug logging (true/false) - shows detailed room management, connections, signaling
DEBUG=true

# Verbose logging (true/false) - shows RTP packet forwarding details
VERBOSE_LOG=false 
# Accept clients that present only the server's shared password, with no signed
# token. Defaults to false -- tokens are required.
#
# That password was never a secret from anybody who had been in a call: the
# server handed it to the browser so the browser could present it back. Holding
# it was enough to open a socket here directly, claim any user id, and enter any
# room, which bypassed the access checks the server had already made.
#
# The only reason to set this is a staged upgrade where this SFU is running
# ahead of servers too old to mint tokens. Set it false for the duration, then
# unset it. Every legacy join logs a warning naming the server, so the log says
# when nothing is relying on it any more.
# SFU_REQUIRE_CLIENT_TOKEN=false

# Where Prometheus metrics are served. Not the main port, on purpose.
#
# /metrics used to sit on the signalling port beside the WebSocket, so anything
# fronting this SFU -- a reverse proxy, a Cloudflare tunnel -- served the whole
# Prometheus register to anybody who asked. Not media and not who is in a call,
# but room and peer counts, Go runtime internals and build information, which is
# more than a stranger needs.
#
# A separate port rather than a token, because a token only protects people who
# set one. The monitoring stack in the Compose files is opt-in, so most
# deployments run no Prometheus at all: a token would have left them exposed and
# given them nothing.
#
# Prometheus reaches this as `sfu:9091` over the Compose network, which needs no
# published port. Publishing it, or running with host networking, puts metrics
# back on the public internet.
#
# 0 switches serving them off entirely; they are still recorded.
# SFU_METRICS_PORT=9091

# The address the metrics port listens on. Unset, it's every interface, which
# Prometheus in another container needs. If the SFU runs straight on the host and
# only that machine reads its metrics, set 127.0.0.1. The desktop app does that.
# It has to be an IP address, or the SFU won't start.
# SFU_METRICS_HOST=127.0.0.1
